		(uuid "0c520d63-17b1-486c-9bda-d39a4be05671")
	)
	(text "Christopher needs three signal levers if the siding is a real siding, \nmeaning a train can be held on it behind its own leaving signals. \nTwo levers only work if the siding is an unsignalled runaround inside the plant. \n\nA three-position signal lever can ask for only one direction at a time, \nand the switch positions then pick which signal on that lever clears. \nSo any two routes that can be lined at the same time must sit on different levers.\n\nWith switch levers 1 (north siding switch), 3 (crossover, both ends) and 5 \n(south siding switch), the two ends of the siding are independent of each other:\n\n- **Crossover reversed:** a southbound train can run Main 2 → crossover → Main 1 \n  while another train enters or leaves the south end of the siding via switch 5. \n  The two routes share no track.\n- **Crossover normal:** three routes can be lined at once: Main 1 straight through, \n  a train entering the north end of the siding, and a train leaving the south end.\n\nIf one lever covered all the Main 2 and siding signals, \"lever South\" with switch 5 \nreversed would call both the south siding leaving signal and the north Main 2 entering \nsignal. The dispatcher could not clear one without the other. Splitting Main 2 into \na north-end lever and a south-end lever removes that ambiguity.\n\n## Lever and mast names\n\n| Lever | Mast | Location | Governs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 | **2SAB** | Main 2, north end | Southward: A = straight on Main 2; B = diverging (crossover to Main 1, or into the siding) |\n| 2 | **2NC** | Siding, north end | Northward out of the siding to Main 2 |\n| 4 | **4SC** | Main 1, north end | Southward, straight only (the crossover is a trailing switch for this move) |\n| 4 | **4NAB** | Main 1, south end | Northward: A = straight on Main 1; B = crossover to Main 2 |\n| 6 | **6NAB** | Main 2, south end | Northward: A = straight on Main 2; B = into the siding |\n| 6 | **6SC** | Siding, south end | Southward out of the siding to Main 2 |\n\nEach lever-and-direction pair calls exactly one mast, so no switch-position selection between masts is needed.\n\n- **Through moves on Main 2:** these use 2S southbound and 6N northbound; the far-end lever stays normal.\n- **A third head on 2S:** if the railroad wanted to distinguish the crossover from the siding entrance, the mast would become 2SABC and the siding leaving signal 2ND.\n- **Leaving signals:** 2NC and 6SC would typically be dwarfs or short masts.\n\n## The two-lever version\n\nIf the siding has no leaving signals, it is just an alternate route inside the plant and only \nthe four outer masts remain. At most two routes can then be lined at once (one per main), so \ntwo levers suffice: 2SAB and 2NCD on Main 2, 4SC and 4NAB on Main 1. The cost is that a train\ncannot be held on the siding while Main 2 is used past it."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
